(* =====================================================================================================
   ONE-TIME USE OF THE LINK, the cryptographic part (PROTOCOL §4). Here A, hypothetically, does NOT burn
   the invite and accepts any number of sessions with one link (in the code, after a correct psk_mac the invite
   burns on any outcome). The link secret has not leaked. The question: can someone else's M1 be replayed?
   - TLS intact (T-tls): psk_mac is bound to the exporter X, and X is unique to each connection, so a replay is impossible (true).
   - TLS compromised (T-null): see replay_tnull.pv: a replay is possible, and only the burning of the invite stops it.
   KCONLY truncation (after key confirmation): the psk_mac check event happens earlier.
   ===================================================================================================== *)

(* EXPECT true — each psk_mac acceptance at A is a separate M1 send at B with the same body *)
query ea: eid, eb: eid, x: exporter, b: bitstring; inj-event(A_PskOk(ea, eb, x, b)) ==> inj-event(B_SentJoin(ea, eb, x, b)).

process
  ( !( new dictA: channel; new matchedA: channel;
       !( new s: linksecret; new eka: esk; new linkch: channel;
          event InviteCreated(epk(eka));
          out(c, epk(eka));
          ( out(linkch, (epk(eka), s))
          | !A_tls(KCONLY, true, false, s, eka, dictA, matchedA)
          | B_tls(KCONLY, true, false, linkch, dictA, matchedA) ) ) ) )
